# ==== T-FMT-C · a comment ABOVE an `else` clause documents the CLAUSE
#
# A comment written on its own line above `else:` documents the BRANCH. With
# no leading-comment hook at the clause site it fell through to
# `format_block_stmts`, which re-emitted it INSIDE the branch body — where it
# documents the first statement instead. Same misattribution class as the
# arm-loop hooks, seven sites further along.
#
# SITE AXIS (7 cells): statement-match `else` · expression-match `else` ·
#   `for` `else` · `while` `else` · `meta match` `else` · `select` `else` · and
#   the ITEM-level `meta if`'s `else`, added when the item-level clause headers
#   turned out to have the whole family's holes (no blank check, no leading
#   hook, no trailing hook) at a layer the original census did not reach.
#   (The `elif` and `if`-`else` cells of the class live in
#   `tests/integration.rs` — `fmt_branch_header_comments_stay_on_the_branch`
#   carries the explicit `elif` cell and REDs on an anchor kill.)
#
# ORDER: blank check -> `emit_comments_before(<clause anchor>)` -> write the
# header, mirroring `format_block_stmts`.
#
# ⚠ CORRECTED: this header used to add "the hooks are LEADING-only — a paired
# trailing hook here would DOUBLE-CLAIM against the header hook the branch
# emitters already write". That reasoning was wrong for `else`, and it is why
# `else:  # note` kept losing its comment into the branch body: `elif` wrote a
# header hook and nothing wrote one for `else`, so there was nothing to
# double-claim against. The indented-`else` sites now emit their own through
# `Formatter::emit_else_header`; the trailing-comment cells live in
# `else_header_trailing_comment.gg`.
#
# What remains true is narrower than the old claim, and than its first
# correction: the LOOP-level hooks in the arm loops are leading-only, because
# the arm's own body emitter writes the trailing half. That emitter is
# `format_arm_body`, NOT `format_match_arm` — the latter used to place the
# header comment itself and no longer does; it delegates, along with the
# expression-match `else`, `catch` and `rethrow`.
#
# RED pre-fix (recorded 2026-08-11): all six statement-level cells pulled the
# comment down into the branch body. The seventh — the ITEM-level `meta if`
# `else` — was added later with the item-level clause work and RED-verified the
# same way, separately: removing its `emit_comments_before` moves
# `# every other platform` from above `else:` to inside the branch, leading the
# first DEFINITION.

void stmt_match_else(int x):
    match x:
        case 1:
            print(1)
        # the fallback
        else:
            print(2)

int expr_match_else(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1:
            10
        # the fallback
        else:
            20
    return r

void for_else():
    for i in 0..3:
        print(i)
    # ran to completion
    else:
        print(9)

void while_else():
    while false:
        print(1)
    # never entered
    else:
        print(2)

String meta_match_else[T]():
    meta match typename(T):
        case "int":
            return "i"
        # every other type
        else:
            return "o"

void select_else():
    Channel[int] ch = Channel[int](1)
    select:
        case int v = ch.recv():
            print(v)
        # nothing ready
        else:
            print(0)
    ch.close()

meta if platform() == "macos":
    int backend_id():
        return 1
# every other platform
else:
    int backend_id():
        return 2

void main():
    stmt_match_else(1)
    print(expr_match_else(1))
    for_else()
    while_else()
    print(meta_match_else[int]())
    select_else()
    print(backend_id())
